From 822d12e06030b628b21f062689f03f18c179405a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 16:08:51 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Show a host's own core update on the version pill
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Desktop only knew about updates it started itself. When hermes-sync updated
the hub on its own (2020-01-01: 12 min draining, 4 min installing), the only
sign was "Waiting for a Hermes update" on a held message.

- GET /api/hermes/update/progress (hermes_cli/core_update_progress.py) reads
  the host's published window.json, drain.json and governor-other-defers.json:
  state (batching/draining/applying/blocked), elapsed on the host's clock,
  the drainer's cap and what it is waiting on; null when none is owed.
- Desktop polls it every 10 s (client pill pinned to the local backend,
  backend pill to the remote host). The pill spins and reads
  "v0.23.1 · updating" with a thin bar filling toward the drainer's cap
  (a sweep once past it or while installing); the tooltip says how long and
  what it waits on. A batched update shows "update in 31m". When the update
  finishes the version check refreshes at once.
- turn_marker: a message queued behind the update hold never replaces a live
  sibling backend's running-turn marker (#00000); hermes-sync now treats
  hold-queue markers as not running, so that marker is what keeps the drain
  from updating over the sibling's turn.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
